  "narrative": "I assessed all due results as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1, a positive signed mean difference means the exposed group has a more negative, stronger dependency score; for direction +1, a positive signed mean difference means a less negative, weaker dependency score. For interactions, a positive signed value supports the registered main-direction effect being stronger in the named subgroup relative to the complement. I used approximately 10% of the absolute population mean dependency score as the practical clinical-significance benchmark, giving rough thresholds near 0.025 for KRAS, EGFR, ERBB2, and DHX9; 0.027 for MDM2; 0.0175 for USP7; 0.0415 for SMARCA2; 0.028 for STAT3; 0.030 for WRN; and smaller for NFE2L2. Acceptance required a clearly directionally positive signed effect and uncertainty consistent with a clinically meaningful difference; rejection of a modifier required the interval to exclude clinically meaningful support in the registered direction. H23 is accepted: TP53 loss versus intact shows a very large positive signed effect for weaker dependency_USP7 in the Avana-library stratum, estimate 0.4046 with 95% Welch interval 0.3299 to 0.4792, well above the USP7 benchmark and replicating the overall and previously validated TP53-loss/USP7 association. H265 is rejected as a registered modifier: the signed interaction estimate for SMARCA4 loss and dependency_SMARCA2 by interferon signature is -0.0242, interval -0.0683 to 0.0200. The upper bound is below the SMARCA2 clinical benchmark and on the opposite side of the registered positive-support direction, so there is no evidence for the proposed clinically meaningful IFN-dependent strengthening; this does not prove exact homogeneity because the interval includes a modest opposite-signed difference. All other due valid results remain unresolved. H259 EGFR amplification versus wild type in KRAS-wild-type high-quality adenocarcinoma shows only a small positive signed effect on dependency_EGFR, 0.0092, interval -0.0444 to 0.0628, so it does not support clinically meaningful receptor-lineage coherence beyond the overall weak signal. H261 KRAS-mutant versus wild type in high-quality high-Cas9 adenocarcinoma is small, 0.0175, interval -0.0225 to 0.0575, and cannot establish self-dependency. H17 and H71 add adenocarcinoma and screen-quality strata for KRAS mutation and are likewise small with intervals including zero and extending above the benchmark, so they remain unresolved and closed. H26 TP53 loss for weaker dependency_MDM2 in adenocarcinoma remains unresolved, estimate 0.0161, interval -0.0142 to 0.0464; this is consistent with the broader pattern that TP53 loss does not robustly predict MDM2 dependency in this dataset. H263 tests MTAP co-loss within CDKN2A-loss, high-quality screens and has a promising point estimate for stronger dependency_MDM2, 0.0767, but a very wide interval, -0.0477 to 0.2010, with n=47 exposed and n=178 comparator; it is not evaluable enough to accept. H264 and H266 test interaction modification of robust associations. USP7 TP53 interaction by proliferation is essentially null, 0.0029, interval -0.1645 to 0.1704; STAT3 PTEN interaction by high serum is positive in point estimate, 0.0470, but very imprecise, -0.0570 to 0.1509. These cannot accept or exclude clinically meaningful heterogeneity, so they are unresolved and closed. H260 and H262 are not evaluable because the joint restrictions produce fewer than 20 observations in one comparison cell. No estimate or interval is available, so these are unresolved and closed as insufficiently powered descriptive probes, not empirical nulls. Audit: exposure variables are genomic or technical predictors rather than the outcome, so direct outcome leakage is unlikely. However, restrictive eligibility filters for screen quality, Cas9 activity, lineage, expression, proliferation, and co-mutations can create selected subpopulations and potential collider-like associations; therefore accepted and unresolved findings are dataset-specific associations within selected strata, not causal effects or externally generalizable claims. Multiplicity is high, and independent validation requests are exhausted. No validation was requested because the remaining voluntary validation budget is zero.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 21 appraise outcome: H23 accepted as a strong internal replication of TP53-loss/weaker-USP7 dependency in Avana-library screens. H265 rejected as a clinically meaningful IFN-signature modifier of SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2. All other new valid comparisons remain unresolved and closed: EGFR-amplification/KRAS-wt/high-quality adeno, KRAS-mutant self-dependency in adeno or high-quality/high-Cas9 contexts, TP53-loss/MDM2 in adenocarcinoma, MTAP co-loss within CDKN2A loss, and interaction tests for USP7/proliferation and STAT3/serum. Jointly restricted probes H260 and H262 were invalid due sparse cells and closed without inferential interpretation. With validation budget exhausted, the evidence-supported dataset-specific associations remain the six robust replicated associations: SMARCA4 loss/stronger SMARCA2, KEAP1 loss/weaker NFE2L2, TP53 loss/weaker USP7, MSI-high/stronger WRN, PTEN loss/stronger STAT3, and CDKN2A loss/stronger MDM2. Remaining uncertainty concerns EGFR amplification, KRAS mutation/NF1-loss small contrasts, DHX9/RB1 weak signals, MTAP co-deletion modification, and interaction heterogeneity. Further internal subgroup slicing is low value; future work should prioritize external well-powered replication of the six accepted associations and targeted testing of EGFR and KRAS/NF1 contrasts."
